/-!
# Equality in the quotient by a pre-equivalence relation (Stacks 046O)

Let `j : R ⟶ U ×_Z U` be a pre-equivalence relation. Two sections of `U` over a test object have
the same image in the quotient sheaf `U/R` exactly when, after an fppf covering, they are the
source and the target of a common section of `R`. Equivalently, the canonical map
`R ⟶ U ×_{U/R} U` is a surjection of fppf sheaves.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.toQuotientSheaf_eq_iff_locally_related`: **Stacks 046O**, the local
  characterization of equality in the quotient.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocallySurjective_relationToQuotientPullback`: **Stacks 046O**, the
  surjectivity of `R ⟶ U ×_{U/R} U`.

## Implementation notes

An fppf covering of a test object is presented by a `CategoryTheory.PreZeroHypercover` whose
induced family is a covering of the big fppf site; `exists_fppf_hypercover_refining_sieve` turns a
covering sieve into such a family, and `fppf_hypercover_sieve_mem` turns such a family back into a
covering sieve. Both go through the saturation of the precoverage, which is how the topology of
the big fppf site is generated.

The forward implication is local injectivity of the unit of sheafification: two sections with the
same image in the sheafification agree on a covering sieve already in the quotient *presheaf*, and
there `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.quotientPresheaf_obj_equiv` turns equality into the equivalence
relation generated by `R`. Being a pre-equivalence relation is what collapses that generated
relation back to one step, which is the content of `related_of_quotientPresheaf_eq`.

The relation is stated in terms of the source first and the target second, matching the order of
the two sections, and the fibre square is formed in presheaves, which computes the fibre square of
sheaves.

Local injectivity of the unit of sheafification is not an instance at these universes, because the
big fppf site has objects one universe above its morphisms; it follows from the site being
essentially small, which is `AlgebraicGeometry.essentiallySmall_bigFppfOver`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 043C](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/043C)
